this is a production of cornell  university  i'm larry smart i'm a professor in the  horticulture section of the school of  integrated plant science  based here at cornell agritech in geneva  so at cornell we started hemp research  in late 2016.  we have been doing  variety evaluations  assessment of disease threats and insect  pest threats  and  doing some work on crop management  especially  disease management and weed management  so with legalization of hemp in the 2014  farm bill  cornell  eagerly took up the challenge of  starting to work on this crop  that has not been studied for decades  and has a high regulatory burden uh for  us as researchers we've been very  fortunate at cornell to have active  engagement across multiple disciplines  to build a  engaged and diverse team of researchers  and extension specialists including  myself as a plant breeder  joss rose  doing biochemistry of cannabinoids which  is critical  to maintain compliance and to breed  compliant cultivars  chris smart and gary bergstrom in plant  pathology  evaluating disease threats  and neil matson of working on controlled  environment production  most recently lynn sosnoski here at  cornell agritech has joined our team  working on herbicide advocacy and weed  management alan taylor is our seed  scientist  studying seed quality  and working on seed coatings  we have been very fortunate to have a  strong collaboration with new york state  department of agriculture and markets  who has given us quite a bit of latitude  for  what i would call self-auditing and  self-regulation  and because of that i think we have been  able to make some major breakthroughs in  our understanding of the genetic basis  of thc and cbd production  which has contributed to  rapid breeding for thc compliance and  truly an understanding of the genetic  basis uh for  uh thc production and thc compliance in  hemp crops  we have also gotten strong support  from new york state with strong  supporters in the legislature and the  governor's office  to initiate the first land-grant  academic  hemp breeding program  so we have been breeding hemp varieties  building a hemp germ plasm collection  and developing markers for marker  assisted selection since early 2017.  our phenotyping is largely based on the  evaluation of current commercial  cultivars  in three market classes  we are very interested in developing  new cultivars for grain production  and the grain will meet  a niche  for  animal feed particularly organic animal  feed we see strong potential  in the poultry market for hemp grain  improving egg quality it is a  gluten-free feed so we see strong  potential for beef  and  actually pet food  production  but it also is a source of protein  and potential ingredient  source for plant-based meats  and also it produces a very healthy oil  which is also used in health and  cosmetic products  so there is a strong potential to  increase  yield of grain production  we're also very interested in the fiber  market and this field behind me  is evaluation in strip trials of some  fiber varieties from china  which flower extremely late which makes  them very good  biomass and fiber producers  we see strong potential  in fiber production  not only for the long strong bass fibers  which  can go into technical applications  reinforced panels  building materials insulation materials  for example but also for the inner herd  the inner core of the stem  which has potential again for  construction materials in the form of  hemp creek but also serves as a very  good absorbent product for animal  bedding and in fact that is one of the  immediate products that has gone  onto the market is for  small animal bedding for your gerbil  cage for example  a lot of potential for further  development of products  for example  absorbent products for diapers  for environmental cleanup  uh and other applications so once we  have sufficient agricultural production  of hemp i think we're going to see  a great market expansion and  diversification of the products that we  can make both from the grain and from  the stem  a lot of the hype around hemp has been  based on the production of cannabinoids  primarily cannabidiol or cbd  so we saw a huge  interest in 2019  from farmers  after uh  cannabis sativa was taken off of  schedule one hemp was taken off of  schedule one by the uh 2018 farm bill  that  released a lot of the regulatory issues  and encouraged farmers to take up the  production of hemp  but that resulted in extreme over  production  estimates uh that we produced as much as  four or five fold more  biomass than the market could bear and  in fact there are still growers who have  material in storage  uh that has not been fully processed  from the 2019 growing season  so prices crashed and we see this sort  of classic characteristic characteristic  roller coaster ride  of over enthusiasm over-production  and then a crash of prices  uh  a disillusionment  and now we are in the process of  diversification of the market  and uh re-engagement of growers  as we continue to improve production  practices and  uh develop new varieties to meet those  needs of the market so the farm bill  authorized us to do research on hemp  hemp and drug type cannabis are a single  same species  cannabis sativa  in fact  we  through some of our work we have  identified that there is a rather simple  genetic basis  for either high thc production  relatively equal mixed production of thc  and cbd  or high cbd production  the interesting phenomenon that we've  characterized in cbd hemp is that the  enzyme cbda synthase  also produces  a certain amount of thc  so every cbd hemp plant also makes a  little bit of thc  we at cornell only work on hemp which is  cannabis sativa that produces less than  0.3 percent percent thc  those regulations have been a patchwork  from state to state and again new york  state has been  rather  a rather compliant state to grow hemp in  because their regulations have been  based on quantification of delta 9 thc  rather than total thc  these are some of the complications and  regulatory burdens that we face  in working with hemp  we've also been very fortunate at  cornell agritech to receive strong  support from usda ars  and in fact through initiatives from  senator schumer's office  ars decided to locate  the new hemp  germplasm repository at cornell agritech  in geneva  so that will be a tremendous advantage  for us in our breeding program to be  able to work side by side with the  curator of the germplasm collection  and  geneticists characterizing that  collection  we will work with them to assemble  material  make sure that it is compliant for thc  so that it can be entered into the  repository  scaled up and then distributed to hemp  breeders  so  for every other crop  that  academic and industrial breeders work on  there is  an extensive and well characterized  germplasm collection  that  used to exist for hemp  uh was assembled back in the 1930s 20s  and 30s  but then was destroyed when hemp was put  on the controlled substance list  so right now we face the process of  reassembling  a diverse germplasm collection and  characterizing it using modern feeding  phenotyping and genotyping tools  so that there is useful  and diverse germplasm  ongoing into the future for industrial  and academic hemp breeders  so this is our fourth year of evaluating  cannabinoid hemp varieties  this trial behind me we have 40  different  cbd and now  cannabidioral cbg varieties that we  evaluate in small plots five plant plots  replicated four times in the field  we're using typical production  practices  for the northeast which is  to plant into raised beds with plastic  mulch for weed management  we put down landscape fabric in the  alleys  so that we don't have to mow and also to  provide a nice uniform background for  drone imaging  so we will be doing active weekly drone  imaging of this trial  to  be able to quantify  nutrient status  any disease and pest incidents  initiation of flowering  and to use lidar to estimate biomass  production and yield  by evaluating these cultivars for the  last four years actually we have  identified trait segregation  because the state of hemp breeding is  still in its infancy for cbd production  in the u.s  so actually many of the quote-unquote  cultivars that we have evaluated have  been segregating some of them  segregating dramatically for  single major gene traits  such as flowering time  so we have been able to  map and identify candidate genes  that determine  day neutrality  hemp is a short day plant  but does have some variants that are day  neutral that will flower after a certain  maturity date  so that is a simple  single gene recessive trait  that is segregating  three to one in f2 populations  we are also very interested in mapping  genes for powdery mildew resistance  powdery mildew is  one of the diseases that has been uh  most  abundant most uh prevalent in the  northeast and in new york it's also an  important  pathogen in controlled environment  production  so if we can identify  genes and molecular markers to  introgress powdery mildew resistance it  would have a big impact on the industry  we by evaluating cultivars year after  year with chris smart  and her lab doing  the powdery mildew evaluations and  characterizing powdery mildew  populations  we have been able to identify some  varieties that show consistent strong  resistance  and have developed f2 mapping  populations which we have in the field  this year  hopefully to identify  genes for either susceptibility or  resistance to powdery mildew  we will also be  sequencing the goal of enemies genome  once we can get sufficient high quality  dna  so in addition to mapping major genes  for powder humidity resistance and for  the day neutral trait we are very  interested in quantifying the  quantitative differences in  critical day length  so there is quite a bit of variation in  flowering time from  late july early august through  early october  which will be represents adaptation to  different latitudes  and  being able to develop cultivars  with the proper  critical day length and flowering time  sensitivity for different latitudes is a  big issue right now  for example there is a lot of interest  in the southeast and in texas  in fiber production  but most of the fiber varieties have  been developed at northerly latitudes  so they flower very early when we bring  them to southern latitudes  also  there are  tropical countries that have been  growing fibre hemp  forever  but do not have access to high cbd hemp  which have been developed  by crossing hemp with  high thc cannabis and then selecting for  high cbd production in those  effectively hybrids  so  there are folks  at equatorial latitudes that are very  interested in having high cbd hemp  which can grow on a 12-hour 12-hour  cycle  that's that's a problem that we have not  solved yet  another trait that we're very interested  in characterizing is the relationship of  cannabinoid production to  insect damage  and  it has long been theorized that  hemp produces cannabinoids thc and cbd  potentially for insect defense  we have a little bit of insight into  that because there are cannabinoid-free  grain and fiber cultivars  and we have seen  that those particular cultivars seem to  be  heavily damaged  by herbivory from japanese beetle  so this  observation has led us to test the  hypothesis that  the production of cannabinoids plays a  role  in  pest interactions perhaps as a defensive  agent or perhaps in  the deterrence of pests from herbivory  we have developed a mapping population  that has variation in cannabinoid  production  some plants are are cannabinoid  competent some are cannabinoid deficient  and we have planted those on multiple  sites and will survey for  relative damage by insect pests in the  field  this has been a production of cornell  university on the web at cornell.edu

