g time from  late july early august through  early october  which will be represents adaptation to  different latitudes  and  being able to develop cultivars  with the proper  critical day length and flowering time  sensitivity for different latitudes is a  big issue right now  for example there is a lot of interest  in the southeast and in texas  in fiber production  but most of the fiber varieties have  been developed at northerly latitudes  so they flower very early when we bring  them to southern latitudes  also  there are  tropical countries that have been  growing fibre hemp  forever  but do not have access to high cbd hemp  which have been developed  by crossing hemp with  high thc cannabis and then selecting for  high cbd production in those  effectively hybrids  so  there are folks  at equatorial latitudes that are very  interested in having high cbd hemp  which can grow on a 12-hour 12-hour  cycle  that's that's a problem that we have not  solved yet  another trait that we're very interested  in characterizing is the relationship of  cannabinoid production to  insect damage  and  it has long been theorized that  hemp produces cannabinoids thc and cbd  potentially for insect defense  we have a little bit of insight into  that because there are cannabinoid-free  grain and fiber cultivars  and we have seen  that those particular cultivars seem to  be  heavily damaged  by herbivory from japanese beetle  so this  observation has led us to test the  hypothesis that  the production of cannabinoids plays a  role  in  pest interactions perhaps as a defensive  agent or perhaps in  the deterrence of pests from herbivory  we have developed a mapping population  that has variation in cannabinoid  production  some plants are are cannabinoid  competent some are cannabinoid deficient  and we have planted those on multiple  sites and will survey for  relative damage by insect pests in the  field  this has been a production of cornell  university on the web at cornell.edu


